    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what did you mean by goblin accountant?? i'm just trying to understand how my down payment is protected. is there a legal reason why they dont just give it to the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The goblin accountant analogy is just a way to illustrate the escrow agent's role in keeping your funds safe. Think of it like a mischievous little creature that's always on the lookout for any sneaky dealings.\n\nNow, about your down payment: the reason escrow agents hold onto your funds is to protect you from the seller's potential \"goblin tactics.\" In the past, there have been cases where sellers have tried to pocket funds meant for repairs or credits. Escrow agents act as a buffer, ensuring that the funds are used for the agreed-upon purposes.\n\nAs for why they don't just give the funds to the seller directly, it's because of the risk of \"goblin accounting\" – where the seller tries to manipulate the funds for their own gain. By holding onto the funds, the escrow agent can verify that the seller is using the funds as intended, and that the transaction is proceeding smoothly.\n\nIn Seattle, the escrow agent is also responsible for ensuring that the seller complies with the terms of the sale, including any repair or credit obligations. This way, you can be confident that your down payment is being used for its intended purpose – to secure your new home.",
